# Proposed by a fixer (2026-10-15, found by random walks); the documenter may edit or drop it.
# Story: at the desk, the big display above and the laptop's below it. Notes is let go across the two, its title bar on the
# upper display and its body on the lower: all of it is to be seen. The lid shuts. macOS brings the window wholly onto the display
# that is left, and there it stays. Arbor used to push it back to where it had been let go, where a strip of it showed, and
# neither ⌃⌥0 nor Show Everything nor a click in the Dock fetched it. Nothing is docked in this story.
# Decision: collab/QUESTIONS.md, Q20 (a floating window's frame is its person's; the one exception is the fit).
# Window ids start at 101.
quiet
displays 2 below
open Notes 800x600
open Mail 600x200
move 102 300 660 600 200
drag 101 700 880
wait 3
loud
expect floating 101
expect visible 101
# The lid shuts. By the time Arbor hears of the displays macOS has moved the window. No hand did that, so it is no new place to Arbor.
macos-moves 101 300 25
displays 1
wait 3
expect visible 101
# Where macOS put it, above Mail, and not merely somewhere in view: a second move would be a jolt.
expect above 101 102
home
wait 3
expect above 101 102
# The lid opens and Notes is let go across the two again. This time nobody rescues it, which no Mac does; Arbor copes anyway:
# of a window that nobody left so, less than half is never all that shows.
displays 2 below
wait 3
drag 101 700 880
wait 3
expect visible 101
displays 1
wait 3
expect visible 101
